WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
A fixed, stained cell is dead.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.560
One picture of it cannot say which cell moved, which event came first, or whether a

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.340
green cell went on to divide.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.160
Film answers three questions: in what order, how fast, and what did this particular

5
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.400
cell do next.

6
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.630
The cell makes its own label.

7
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.150
A protein of interest is tagged with a genetically encoded fluorescent protein.

8
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:40.630
Fluorescence from green fluorescent protein (GFP) was shown in E. coli and in

9
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:47.590
C. elegans in 1994. A fluorescence microscope separates the excitation light from the

10
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.450
longer wavelength light the label gives off, so the tagged protein stands out

11
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.790
against a dark background.

12
00:00:54.633 --> 00:01:01.893
The living cells or animals sit on the microscope stage, kept alive: cells in a

13
00:01:01.893 --> 00:01:07.133
dish, brain slices, a transparent larva, or an anaesthetised animal.

14
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:12.233
The same field is imaged again and again at a chosen interval.

15
00:01:13.493 --> 00:01:16.893
Chromosomes in human cells were imaged every 30 seconds.

16
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:20.313
Arabidopsis ovules, every 5 minutes.

17
00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.893
Brain slices and cultures, every few minutes.

18
00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:28.717
Then each cell is followed from frame to frame.

19
00:01:29.257 --> 00:01:35.917
This is tracking. Its colour, its shape changes and its division are read as one

20
00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:40.617
history. Two FUCCI reporters can mark the cell cycle.

21
00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:44.477
A red nucleus is in G0 or G1.

22
00:01:44.477 --> 00:01:49.037
A green nucleus is in S, G2 or M.

23
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.207
A movie is read as a sequence.

24
00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:59.247
Take the position of each tracked cell in every frame, then measure a rate, distance

25
00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:01.907
over time, or an order of events.

26
00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:10.447
On a cell cycle movie, red, then green, then a rounded dividing cell, then two dim

27
00:02:10.447 --> 00:02:13.147
daughters marks one completed cycle.

28
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:17.207
A green cell alone is not a completed cycle.

29
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.460
Imaging itself costs the cell.

30
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:26.280
Illumination damages cellular molecules and can impair physiology or kill the

31
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.820
sample, in ways that are not always visible in its shape.

32
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.860
Limit illumination, for example to the focal plane, and test for phototoxicity

33
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.520
effects. A single frame can also mislead about fate.

34
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.800
A lagging chromosome in one frame does not give the daughter's final chromosome

35
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.840
count. Follow it until its fate is clear.

36
00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:57.273
In 2001, Noctor, Kriegstein and colleagues labelled a few cells in embryonic rat

37
00:02:57.273 --> 00:03:01.013
cortex with a retrovirus carrying a fluorescent protein.

38
00:03:02.093 --> 00:03:06.393
Time-lapse images showed that proliferative radial glia generate neurons.

39
00:03:07.373 --> 00:03:10.573
A fixed section cannot show who gave birth to whom.

40
00:03:11.713 --> 00:03:18.753
In 2005, Rink, Ghigo, Kalaidzidis and Zerial filmed single endosomes.

41
00:03:19.473 --> 00:03:24.553
Rab5 was replaced by Rab7 while the cargo stayed in the same endosome.

42
00:03:25.253 --> 00:03:26.513
That supports maturation.

43
00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:34.997
In 2015, Hines, Appel and colleagues filmed myelin wrapping in zebrafish.

44
00:03:36.017 --> 00:03:40.777
About 75% of wrapping attempts disappeared within 90 minutes.

45
00:03:42.117 --> 00:03:46.257
Wraps already formed on silenced axons retracted more often.

46
00:03:47.317 --> 00:03:49.477
Retraction is only seen in a movie.

47
00:03:50.317 --> 00:03:56.097
Keep one rule. A movie shows what those cells did, not what all cells do.
